cctools-as 806 for OPENSTEP 4.2/i386

Install with: sh ./pkg install cctools-as
Test with:    sh ./pkg test cctools-as

Use the assembler directly:
    /usr/local/bin/as-806 -o example.o example.s

Select it explicitly with Apple GCC 4.2 (5666.3):
    gcc-4.2 -specs=/usr/local/lib/cctools-as/gcc42.specs example.c -o example
    g++-4.2 -specs=/usr/local/lib/cctools-as/gcc42.specs example.cc -o example

The specs cover normal C, C++, Objective-C, Objective-C++, and assembly
compilation, including -pipe. GCC still reports /bin/as with
-print-prog-name=as; use -v to see the assembler actually invoked.
Without the specs option GCC continues to use the stock assembler.
Apple's optional PCH repository path (-fsave-repository) still uses stock as.
The linker, system tools, compiler ABI, and CPU options are unchanged.

Only the i386 backend is installed. Instruction encoding support does not
imply runtime CPU or operating-system support. In particular, keep SSE/SSE2
disabled unless kernel XMM-state support has been independently established.

The build uses upstream's assembler makefile and seven private libstuff
source files, with package-local integer/header compatibility fixes. Local
section differences use the older scattered SECTDIFF relocation pair that
stock OPENSTEP ld understands; normal dynamic object output is retained.
No configure script, replacement system headers, or support libraries are
installed. The pristine source is fetched with a pinned SHA-256 checksum;
cctools-806-openstep.patch contains the source changes.

Validated on 2026-10-06, OPENSTEP 4.2/i386 at 192.168.1.76:
  - pkg build, pkg install, and pkg test passed as user me, without su.
  - The installed test checks instruction bytes without executing SIMD,
    global/local section differences, .quad, mixed stock/new objects,
    direct assembly and GCC specs selection, -pipe, constructors,
    C++ exceptions, Objective-C and Objective-C++ runtime calls.
  - The existing gcc42/test suite also passed using temporary compiler
    wrappers selecting this assembler, including the Objective-C return ABI.
  - The source patch applies cleanly to pristine cctools-806.
  - Compilation used upstream's assembler makefile with /bin/gnumake 3.74;
    configure was not needed. Unused architecture packing and visibility
    warnings were harmless. Transfer extraction reported clock-skew timestamp
    warnings; package installation itself completed without those warnings.
